--- !u!114 &218592348
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 218592346}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f4688fdb7df04437aeb418b961361dc5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: "Message to all 6/12 gas station employees,\r\n\r\nHey everyone, this is
    your manager, Derek. I\u2019ve heard several complaints from customers and staff
    that the slushy machines are old and no longer freeze before opening. While this
    site doesn\u2019t have the funds to buy a new machine, I watched an interesting
    YouTube video from a channel called Be Smart (2026) that says heating something
    up can make it freeze faster.\r\n\r\nNow, I know you can\u2019t just believe
    everything you see on the internet, so I did a quick search and found a research
    paper that describes this \u201CMpemba effect\u201D (Burridge & Hallstadius,
    2020), so it seems real.\r\n\r\nAnd so it occurred to me that if heating water
    always makes it freeze faster, then heating our slushies before we put them in
    the machine will also make them freeze faster, right? \r\n\r\nSo from now on,
    when you\u2019re on opening shift, please heat the slushy liquid to near-boiling
    before putting it in the machine and turning it on. If you do it right, I don\u2019t
    expect to see any more complaints about this issue.\r\n\r\nYour direct supervisor
    and manager,\r\nDerek"
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
  m_sharedMaterial: {fileID: 2180264, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
