  m_text: Monotremes are mammals that lay eggs instead of giving birth to live young.
    Another unusual trait shared by monotremes is electrolocation, the ability to
    sense and locate the electrical fields living things emit when their muscles
    contract. For example, the platypus uses electrolocation to detect prey in water
    while its eyes, ears, and nostrils are closed. Its duck-like bill contains thousands
    of sensory receptors that detect tiny electrical signals generated by prey.
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
