    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KLmtn_xwgS4

    

    Summery:

    The
    video "Why Can Hot Water Sometimes Freeze Faster?" by Be Smart explains the Mpemba
    effect, the surprising phenomenon in which hot water can sometimes freeze faster
    than cold water.

    

    The effect first drew scientific attention in 1963,
    when a Tanzanian student named Erasto Mpemba noticed that his hot ice cream mixture
    froze faster than his classmates'' cooler mixtures.

    

    Scientists have
    found that the effect is difficult to reproduce because many factors can affect
    freezing, including evaporation, convection, dissolved gases, and supercooling.

    

    The
    video also explains that the Mpemba effect is not limited to water. Similar effects
    have been observed in materials, magnets, and even quantum physics.

    

    Scientists
    suggest that systems starting farther from equilibrium can sometimes reach a
    stable state faster by following different pathways.

    

    Overall, the
    video shows that hot water does not always freeze faster than cold water, but
    under certain conditions it can. It also demonstrates how everyday observations
    can lead to important scientific discoveries.

'
